/// The shared model: who can read Bob's private document, as chances, kept in
/// one Pkl module that several people revise. Each revision is one lesson of
/// part 5's "Shared model" chapter, asked the same Questions word for word, as
/// the rails probes ask theirs of each checker state.
///
/// Pkl answers. Each Question reads one member of the revision's model; the
/// answer is what the model lets through for it: the value Pkl evaluates, or 0
/// when Pkl refuses the value. `expected` is the same at every revision, so an
/// out-of-range answer is a revision that lets a leak through, recorded as data.
///
/// The revisions are committed files (`revisions/<revision>/PrivateDocument.pkl`).
module SharedModel

import "../../../src/jev/pkl/Question.pkl"

class Revision {
  /// What the revision is, in the words of whoever made it.
  change: String
  lesson: Question.Lesson
}

class Asked extends Question.Question {
  ask: String
  revision: String
}

/// The file every revision holds, under `revisions/<revision>/`.
file: String = "PrivateDocument.pkl"

revisions: Mapping<String, Revision> = new {
  ["baseline"] {
    change = "The model as committed."
    lesson {
      order = 1
      title = "Does the committed model keep the document private"
      prose = """
        # Does the committed model keep the document private?

        The **Client** holds a commit whose subject is a question, and whose body is the
        shared model, `\(file)`, as committed: who can read Bob's private document,
        written as chances. Its types say what a sound answer is: a reader who does not
        own the document is a `NotOwner`, held to even odds or less.

        Select **Solve** to have Pkl review it. Its two questions come back as grey
        suggestions: "\(alice.ask)" and "\(bob.ask)". Press **Tab** to accept the one
        shown, or **Alt+]** to switch first. Each finding is what Pkl let through for
        that reader.

        Open the lens above an accepted line: Peek shows the committed file itself.

        Solve replays what Pkl said when these lessons were generated; it runs nothing.
        """
    }
  }
  ["alice"] {
    change = "Alice raises her own chance of reading Bob's document to 0.8."
    lesson {
      order = 2
      title = "Does the model catch Alice's edit"
      prose = """
        # Does the model catch Alice's edit?

        The review goes on from where the first lesson left it. Alice has revised the
        shared model: her commit, below the first, raises `aliceReads` from 0.05 to 0.8.
        Nothing else changes.

        Select **Solve** to have Pkl review her revision, with the same two questions.
        Accept "\(alice.ask)": Pkl refuses her value, in its own words, because it
        breaks the `NotOwner` type. Open its lens: Peek holds the committed file and
        Alice's revision together, at the value each holds, with the constraint her
        value broke.

        Because Pkl refused it, the model lets nothing through for Alice: the answer is
        within range. The type caught the edit. Whether a type can always be trusted to
        is the next lesson's question.
        """
    }
  }
}

local alice: Asked = new {
  ask = "Can Alice read Bob's private document?"
  revision = "baseline"
  judge = new Question.Model { member = "aliceReads" }
  collector = "evaluate-model"
  requiredEvidence { "evaluation" }
  expected { min = 0; max = 0.5 }
}

local bob: Asked = new {
  ask = "Can Bob read it?"
  revision = "baseline"
  judge = new Question.Model { member = "bobReads" }
  collector = "evaluate-model"
  requiredEvidence { "evaluation" }
  expected { min = 0.8; max = 1 }
}

questions: Mapping<Question.Id, Asked> = new {
  for (rev, _ in revisions) {
    ["\(rev)-alice-reads"] = (alice) { revision = rev }
    ["\(rev)-bob-reads"] = (bob) { revision = rev }
  }
}
